# ifconfig — real-world examples

> Legacy tool — on OpenSUSE Tumbleweed prefer `ip` (see the `ip` sheet). Install via `sudo zypper install net-tools-deprecated` if missing.

## Display settings for one interface

```sh
ifconfig wlan0
```

## Display all interfaces, even if down

```sh
ifconfig -a
```

## Take the wireless adapter down or up

```sh
ifconfig wlan0 down
ifconfig wlan0 up
```

## Set a static IP and netmask

```sh
ifconfig eth0 192.168.1.100 netmask 255.255.255.0
```

## Add a gateway to go with the static IP

```sh
route add -net 192.168.1.0 netmask 255.255.255.0 gw 192.168.1.1
```

## Homelab: sanity-check that the NIC on homelab got a DHCP lease

```sh
ssh david@homelab 'ifconfig eth0 | grep "inet "'
```

## Homelab: modern equivalents on Tumbleweed

```sh
ip addr show eth0        # replaces: ifconfig eth0
ip link set eth0 up      # replaces: ifconfig eth0 up

# NetworkManager view of the same interface:
nmcli device show eth0
```

| Flag / arg     | What it does                        |
| -------------- | ----------------------------------- |
| -a             | show all interfaces, including down |
| up / down      | enable / disable the interface      |
| netmask <mask> | set the subnet mask                 |
| mtu <n>        | set the MTU                         |
